## Lead 4: GraphQL Schema Leak in Public JS Bundle

**Asset:** `app.alfaview.com/js/app.min.5b3949112f0cf682adc8.js`

| Gate | Answer |
|------|--------|
| Q1 In scope? | **Yes** — app.alfaview.com is in-scope |
| Q2 Reachable? | **Yes** — 1.09MB JS bundle served publicly without auth |
| Q3 Impact? | **Limited** — full admin GraphQL schema (60+ mutations, 45+ queries) is embedded; enables full API surface mapping; however, introspection is disabled server-side and resolvers are auth-gated |
| Q4 Provable? | **Yes** — public JS bundle contains full schema with argument types |
| Q5 Novel? | **No** — client-side schema embedding in SPAs is a well-known information disclosure pattern; out-of-scope per "reports resulting from automated scanning utilities without additional details" |
| Q6 Always-rejected? | **Borderline** — "descriptive error messages or headers" and "publicly accessible login panels" are rejected; schema leak in JS is similar class |
| Q7 Reasonable triager? | **No** — OWASP lists this as informational; most programs reject without concrete exploitation |

**Verdict: INVALID**

- **Reason:** Informational disclosure only; no concrete exploit demonstrated; schema is embedded in client-side code (expected behavior for SPAs); program explicitly rejects scanner-only reports without POC.

