[HYP] Guest-link and group-link updates are the only link mutations reachable without room context, and the sibling read returns the guest join credential
class: IDOR
asset: apis.alfaview.com — GET and PATCH /v2/guest-links/{id}, PATCH /v2/group-links/{id}
confidence: 62
reasoning: Path inventory from the live spec (md5 357b94d3, 37 paths) shows all link creates and deletes are room-scoped — POST /v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links, DELETE /v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links/{id}, POST /v2/rooms/{roomId}/group-links, DELETE /v2/rooms/{roomId}/group-links/{id} — while both updates are bare: PATCH /v2/guest-links/{id} and PATCH /v2/group-links/{id} carry no roomId or companyId. The handler therefore cannot derive scope from the URL and must resolve UUID to room to tenant before authorizing; that resolve-then-check ordering is the IDOR-prone shape. The sibling read is credential-bearing: GuestLink.required = [id, roomId, createdBy, createdAt, permissionGroupId, accessKey, emailAddress, locale, origin, dialInAllowed, dialInCode, joinUrl], and AuthenticateGuestLinkRequestBody.required = [companyId, roomId, accessKey] with accessKey described as "The access key from the guest link" — so the disclosed accessKey is a direct input to the documented guest authentication flow, and companyId is already public in the app.alfaview.com bundle (01FDY0986YK1BJF2K0F9DXR8EB per standing record). The write body compounds it: GuestLinkUpdate permits emailAddress, permissionGroupId, validFrom, validUntil, dialInAllowed and required sendEmail ("an information email with the updated guest link information will be sent to the associated email address"), i.e. one call can rebind the mailbox, choose the permission tier, and remove the expiry. Every probe returned 401/107B unauthenticated, so no bypass is claimed. The spec's omission of 403 on this operation is NOT used as evidence: I proved this cycle that the spec under-declares auth codes (see REJECTED below).
evidence_needed: With a self-owned guest link L in tenant A and a second tenant B account: PATCH /v2/guest-links/L returns 204 to B, or 204 to A for a guest link belonging to a room A cannot administer. Either converts the hypothesis. A 403/404 closes it.
verify_steps: Read-only now, no third-party data — HEAD https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/guest-links/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000 (expect 401/107B, confirms pre-auth gate unchanged). Decisive step needs two self-owned tenants: GET own /v2/guest-links to list L, then GET and PATCH /v2/guest-links/{L} using tenant B's token with sendEmail:false and a single cosmetic field (displayName) to avoid notifying anyone; 204 proves the cross-tenant write, 403/404 closes it. Never read tenant B's response body.
impact: If the scope check is absent, any authenticated account in any tenant can (a) read another tenant's guest-link accessKey, joinUrl, dialInCode, roomId and associated email, then authenticate through the documented guest-link flow and enter that room; and (b) persist access by rebinding emailAddress to an attacker mailbox, assigning a chosen permissionGroupId, and setting validUntil far future — a standing cross-tenant room-access and invitation-hijack primitive on a tenant-boundary resource. CRITICAL if proven, currently zero. The reportable core today is the design asymmetry, which is provable without any credential.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

