reasoning: This cycle's exhaustive 37-path `Allow:`-versus-spec diff is the strongest evidence yet and it is negative for severity: /v2/users/invitation is spec=[POST] live=[DELETE, POST], and it is the ONLY divergence among 37 paths. Every other 405-emitting route matches its declared methods exactly (api-key, group-link, guest-link, password, meetings cancellation, room-scoped group-links/{id}, guest-links/{id}, permissions/{userId}, subrooms/{subroomId}, users/{id}). The plural /v2/users/invitations is absent from all 37 spec paths and answers `allow: DELETE` only, same 19B Go-native text/plain envelope and same `server: edge-proxy` as the declared route, while declared app routes return application/problem+json 401 — a distinct runtime, replicated byte-identically on beta-apis. Both answer 405 before any 401, so method resolution precedes authentication. Authorization on the DELETE itself remains unmeasured: I did not issue a DELETE.
evidence_needed: One credential-free `DELETE /v2/users/invitations` returning anything other than 401/403 — 202, 204, or a 404-by-id all count as reachable pre-auth. A 401 closes the authorization question and leaves only the spec drift.
verify_steps: HEAD https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/users/invitation (expect 405, allow: DELETE, POST) and HEAD https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/users/invitations (expect 405, allow: DELETE); diff both against the spec method list. Read-only, HEAD only. No DELETE.
impact: Unproven authorization-wise and the exhaustive diff now shows it is a one-off, not a pattern. Independently certain: the public contract understates the destructive surface of a tenant-boundary onboarding resource, so integrators, scoped testers and WAF allow-lists inherit a false map. MEDIUM as spec integrity, not as an access-control finding.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

[HYP] OAuth token introspection client authentication is unenforced while the same build enforces token validation on userinfo
class: AUTH
asset: sso.alfaview.com/oauth2/introspect
confidence: 94
reasoning: Unchanged at 29 cycles and re-confirmed passively only. Liveness: OPTIONS → 405. Metadata 200/2169B with `introspection_endpoint` null while the endpoint is live, and `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported` = [client_secret_basic, client_secret_post, none] — a secret-bearing method is advertised and, per the standing record, never verified at runtime. Same-deployment control: /oauth2/userinfo in the same FusionAuth 1.63.0 build tiers correctly and rejects all five forged JOSE inputs with the byte-identical `access_token_failed_processing`, so the introspect omission is a missing check, not an architectural choice. No POST differentials re-fired this cycle; I did not submit any token.
evidence_needed: A self-owned, self-issued token submitted with a fabricated client_id returning 200 with a populated token body, proving response data reaches an unauthenticated caller.
verify_steps: OPTIONS https://sso.alfaview.com/oauth2/introspect (expect 405, endpoint alive) and GET https://sso.alfaview.com/.well-known/openid-configuration, reading `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported` and `introspection_endpoint`. GET/OPTIONS only, no POST, no token.
impact: Anyone holding one captured token reads its liveness, expiry and scope with no client credential, so revocation cannot be triaged and tokens sort offline; the advertised client_secret_basic gives integrators false assurance. MEDIUM-HIGH, and the highest-confidence weakness in the program.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

[PARKED] Spec response-code omissions as an authorization signal (was carrying ~10 points of H1): `GET /v2/permission-groups` documents only 200/400/404 with no 401 and no 403, and the live server returns 401/107B with `www-authenticate: AccessToken` — identical to the control. The spec demonstrably under-declares auth codes, so the four unscoped operations that omit 403 (`PATCH /v2/guest-links/{id}`, `PATCH /v2/group-links/{id}`, `POST /v2/meetings/{id}/cancellation`, `DELETE /v2/users/{id}`) prove nothing about the code. Signal withdrawn; H1 confidence set on the scoping and credential facts alone.
[PARKED] apis.alfaview.com pre-auth validation ordering (84 conf, score 5.85): fully mapped at 9 of 26 GET ops, bounded, replicated on beta, and demonstrably not an auth bypass since every valid-parameter and every path-param request still returns 401. Passively exhausted and stable for 31 cycles; retains reporting value but produced no new information this cycle, so it does not displace the guest-link hypothesis.
[PARKED] tools.alfaview.com/whiteboard/ (35): my own three-point differential disproved its only actionable property, the code:5 flat catch-all means no method oracle. Re-probed 404/47B unchanged; no reason to re-rank.
[FINAL] H1 unscoped guest/group-link mutation with credential-bearing sibling read — 62 (specific route, specific credential chain, one tenant-token away from resolution)
[FINAL] H2 undeclared destructive DELETE on /v2/users/invitation{,s} — 70 (exhaustively confirmed as the sole 37-path divergence; authz unmeasured)
[FINAL] H3 introspect client authentication unenforced — 94 (standing, passively re-confirmed, no regression)

[NEXT] SCAN: `HEAD https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/group-links/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000` and `HEAD https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/rooms/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/guest-links` to pin whether the unscoped link route is the only member of its family that a bare UUID resolves — i.e. whether `GET /v2/group-links/{id}` and `GET /v2/guest-links/{id}` both 401 while their room-scoped siblings 401 identically (confirming the divergence is in the spec's scoping, not in the gateway), then read the 14 undeclared-path candidates from `components` for any second unscoped `{id}` route. Read-only, HEAD/GET only, <=1 rps.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: all link creates and deletes are room-scoped (POST/DELETE /v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links, /v2/rooms/{roomId}/group-links) but both updates are bare — `PATCH /v2/guest-links/{id}` and `PATCH /v2/group-links/{id}` carry no roomId or companyId, so scope must be resolved from the UUID before authorizing. The unscoped sibling read is credential-bearing: `GuestLink.required` includes `accessKey`, `joinUrl` and `dialInCode`, and `accessKey` is a documented required input to `POST /v2/auth/guest-link`. The update body additionally permits `emailAddress` + `permissionGroupId` + `validFrom`/`validUntil` + `sendEmail` in one call. Unproven — every probe returned 401 — but it replaces a generic 27-cycle "UUID path params" note with a specific route and a specific impact chain.
[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ apis.alfaview.com: the spec's missing 403 responses cannot be used as evidence of missing authorization checks. `GET /v2/permission-groups` declares only 200/400/404 — no 401, no 403 — yet returns 401/107B with `www-authenticate: AccessToken`, identical to the `/v2/rooms` control. Positive control confirms the spec under-declares auth codes, so the four unscoped operations omitting 403 prove nothing. My strongest sub-argument last cycle is withdrawn.
[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ apis.alfaview.com: SSRF closed as a class on the REST surface. Across all 37 operations and every `components.schemas` entry, the only `format: uri` fields are `ErrorModel.instance` and `ErrorModel.type` — RFC 7807 documentation links, not caller input. Zero URL-typed request parameters exist, so there is no server-side fetch primitive to redirect at 169.254.169.254.
[LEARN] REJECTED AUTH @ apis.alfaview.com: mass assignment closed on `PATCH /v2/users`. `UserUpdate` is `additionalProperties: false` with only seven cosmetic fields (city, firstName, initials, lastName, locale, title) and no `id`, `emailAddress`, `permissions` or tenant field, so profile PATCH cannot be used for privilege or identity escalation. Notably the platform does constrain update schemas where it chose to — which sharpens, rather than excuses, the guest-link contrast.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ apis.alfaview.com: queued `Allow:`-versus-spec scan executed across all 37 paths and returned exactly one divergence — `/v2/users/invitation` spec=[POST] live=[DELETE, POST]. The eleven other 405-emitting routes match their declared method sets precisely, so the undeclared-destructive-method class is a single isolated route rather than a systemic contract gap. The scan also proves HEAD is routed to the GET handler on 24 paths (401 or 422, never 405), which confirms the 405s are genuine method rejections and makes the `Allow:` headers reliable.
[LEARN] NO_DELTA @ sso/apis/app/tools: 33rd consecutive stable cycle. OpenAPI md5 357b94d3 (127532B, 37 paths), OIDC 200/2169B (issuer=acme.com, `introspection_endpoint` null, `password`+`client_credentials` advertised), JWKS 200/16257B (7×RS256, RSA-only against 9 advertised algs), introspect OPTIONS 405, authorize 200/6176B, users/me 401/107B, /v2/users/invitations 405 allow:DELETE, graphql GET 400/406B, tools poll 501/55B, /whiteboard/ 404/47B, /health/ 200/615B, client-diagnostics /health 200/16B — every probe byte-identical to the prior cycle, no new endpoints and no regressions.

[RISK] alfaview: 64 — down from 66. Two reductions, one non-additive. First, the exhaustive 37-path `Allow:` diff I queued last cycle came back with exactly one divergence, which proves the undeclared-destructive-method surface is an isolated route rather than the systemic contract gap the +10 last cycle was partly built on. Second, and more important, I killed my own best new argument: the 403-omission asymmetry across the four unscoped operations, which I had folded into the guest-link hypothesis, is disproven by a positive control — the spec under-declares auth codes for `/v2/permission-groups` where the code does enforce 401 — so H1 is scored at 62 on the scoping and credential facts alone and adds nothing to risk while unproven. Offsetting that, the guest-link hypothesis is genuinely the sharpest lead the program has produced in 34 cycles: it converts a vague blanket IDOR note into one specific route whose sibling read returns `accessKey`/`joinUrl`/`dialInCode` as required fields, where `accessKey` is a documented input to the guest authentication flow, and whose update body can rebind the mailbox, choose the permission tier and remove the expiry in a single call — a cross-tenant room-access chain one self-owned account away from resolution. Meanwhile the SSRF and mass-assignment classes closed on the REST surface with zero false positives, which is a real reduction in untested surface. Introspection at 94 remains the highest-confidence weakness, unchanged and un-regressed. Discipline held: 60 requests, all GET/HEAD/OPTIONS at ~1.3s, no DELETE or other mutating verb attempted, no 200 and no data body obtained anywhere, every path parameter a zero UUID, no rate-limit or enumeration testing, and the sole response body read was my own 401 on `/v2/permission-groups`.
