[NEXT] SCAN: complete the unauthenticated recognized-query-parameter map with a bounded GET-only sweep over the 9 query-param GET operations, using the guaranteed-422 trick. For each route, issue one request per candidate parameter with a wrong-typed value, and where a candidate might be hidden, append it **together with** a known-bad declared parameter so the route always returns 422 and the candidate's presence or absence in the same `errors` array is the signal. Example already executed: `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/guest-links?roomId=abc&limit=abc` → 422 with `errors` = `[{"message":"invalid integer","location":"query.limit"}]` only. Targets, in order of value: `GET /v2/guest-links?includeArchived=abc&limit=abc`, `?search=abc&limit=abc`, `?sort=abc&limit=abc`; the same three on `/v2/group-links`; then `/v2/meetings?limit=abc`, `/v2/rooms/{roomId}/attendances?limit=abc`, `/v2/rooms?roomTypes=department&limit=abc` to confirm the enum whitelist and the scalar binder on each remaining route. Record only the `location` values, never any response data, no credential, no iteration, ≤1 rps. A `location` entry not present in the 37-path contract is a genuine new finding — a hidden filter on a credential-bearing list; a clean map matching the contract on every route downgrades the oracle to informational and closes the sweep for good.

**[STEP 6 — LEARNING]**

[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ apis.alfaview.com: a pre-authentication query-parameter binder is a working unauthenticated parameter-enumeration oracle. Append a wrong-typed value to any GET route: a recognized, format-constrained parameter returns 422 naming `location: query.<param>`; an unrecognized one returns the uniform 401/107B md5 `60ed2f29`. Proven same-session on `/v2/guest-links` (`?limit=abc` → 422/141B md5 `551221c3` vs bare → 401), reproduced on `/v2/group-links` with a byte-identical body, and extended to the array class by `/v2/rooms?roomTypes=abc` → 422/190B `"expected value to be one of \"department, room, meeting\""` at `query.roomTypes[0]`. Mechanism is asymmetric: `/v2/rooms/abc` → 401/107B proves path-parameter validation sits behind the auth middleware while query binding does not, and `components.securitySchemes` being absent from the contract is why.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: the company-wide guest-link list is un-narrowable, and un-narrowability is now a **measured server-side fact** rather than a reading of the documentation. `?roomId=abc&limit=abc` and `?companyId=abc&limit=abc` both return 422/141B md5 `551221c3` whose `errors` array contains exactly one entry, `query.limit`; because that array is a complete per-parameter enumerator, its silence on `roomId` and `companyId` proves the server does not bind them. The credential-bearing payload and the missing authorization primitive are unchanged: `GuestLink` requires `accessKey`, `joinUrl`, `dialInCode` and `emailAddress` on 12 of 15 fields, `dialInCode` is required unconditionally beside the required `dialInAllowed`, and `TokenUserPermissions` is a closed `additionalProperties:false` 7-boolean vocabulary with no guest-link axis while every other company-wide read is name-gated. Impact and the token-gated verification path are unchanged; only the strength of the scope half moved.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED AUTH @ apis.alfaview.com: a fourth unauthenticated credential-exchange endpoint exists and had no entry in 34 cycles of notes — `POST /v2/auth/api-key`, `AuthenticateAPIKey`, body `APICredentials` (`clientId`, `companyId`, `key`, all required, `additionalProperties:false`). Established with `HEAD` → 405 `allow: POST` and `OPTIONS` → 405/19B `text/plain` `server: edge-proxy`, so liveness is proven with no POST fired. It is the only auth endpoint declaring a 403 account-status tier ("e.g., account is inactive") that guest-link, group-link and password do not, and API keys are a distinct long-lived per-company trust class from a user password or a guest join credential.
[LEARN] REJECTED AUTH @ apis.alfaview.com — my own prior-cycle claim, retracted: the statement that "the auth gate runs at the edge proxy ahead of query validation, so 422 is impossible on this path and passive resolution is provably exhausted" is **disproved by my own probe this cycle**. `?limit=abc` on the same route returns 422 pre-auth. The error was methodological: I tested only non-schema parameters (`roomId`, `bogusParam`) and therefore had no positive control, so I could not tell an unrecognized parameter from a broken method, and I generalized to a false global rule about a mechanism I had actually only characterized for path parameters and unknown parameters. The correct rule is narrow: schema-declared format-constrained **query** parameters validate pre-auth; path parameters and unrecognized parameters do not. Any earlier conclusion resting on the false global rule needs re-checking, and the "passive avenues exhausted" claim for H1 is withdrawn.
[LEARN] MISCONFIG @ apis.alfaview.com (contract, supporting, not a standalone finding): the `roomTypes` query parameter's own description instructs clients to use `'departments'`, while the accepted enum value is `department` — a developer following the published documentation literally is rejected with 422 `"expected value to be one of \"department, room, meeting\""`. Folded into the oracle finding as evidence of documentation/implementation divergence; the descriptive-error class on its own is rejected.

**[STEP 7 — RISK]**

[RISK] alfaview: 72 — up 4 from 68. The increase is one genuinely new attack primitive rather than a new route: an unauthenticated parameter-enumeration oracle over the whole 37-operation contract of a multi-tenant video API, which is a precondition enabler for every IDOR and business-logic chain in this program, and which in one cycle converted H1's scope half from an inference drawn from documentation into a measured server-side negative. It cost 9 requests to establish and revealed a fourth unauthenticated credential-exchange endpoint that 34 cycles of notes never recorded, with a response tier class (`403` account status) that no sibling auth endpoint declares. Two offsets, and one correction I am obliged to state rather than bury. Negative: nothing was exposed. All 9 requests were GET, HEAD or OPTIONS, all returned 401, 422 or 405, no 200, no response body carried any guest, user, room or tenant data, no reflection beyond the field name `query.limit` and `query.roomTypes[0]`, no credential was used or sought, no account was created, no mutating verb was issued, and the 422 bodies came from the application's own validator on synthetic values. Discipline also cost me a claim: I had to retract my own prior-cycle conclusion that the auth gate preceded all parsing, because a single positive control I failed to run last cycle turned it into a false global rule about a mechanism I had only ever seen one facet of. A finding that rests on a rule I asserted rather than measured is worth less than no finding, and a program whose standing hypothesis list contains one is worth correcting loudly. Introspection at 94 remains the highest-confidence weakness; the oracle at 78 is the highest-value new primitive; the decisive steps on H1, api-key and the admin surface all still gate on one self-owned, email-verified account, which is where this stops being mine to run.
