## STEP 6 — LEARNING

[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: the company-wide guest-link list is un-narrowable, and this cycle closes the *typed-parameter* half of that claim on all four link list routes. The pre-auth binder is proven **per-operation and complete** — `limit` binds on `/v2/guest-links` but not on `/v2/meetings` or `/v2/permission-groups`, `from` binds on `/v2/meetings` but not on `/v2/rooms` — so the absence of an entry in the 422 `errors` array is now a positive measurement rather than an inference from documentation. The room-scoped siblings return the byte-identical 422/141B md5 `551221c3`, so the proof is no longer confined to the two unscoped lists. Two new structural facts sharpen the impact: the four link lists permit `maximum: 500` per page against `/v2/rooms`' `maximum: 50` — a 10x harvest rate on the only responses that carry `accessKey`, `joinUrl`, `dialInCode` and guest `emailAddress` — and the platform demonstrably ships narrowing filters elsewhere (`roomTypes`, `emailAddress`, `from`/`to`), so the absence of any filter across all ten credential-bearing routes is a choice, not an oversight.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ apis.alfaview.com: the pre-auth query-parameter binder as a standalone unauthenticated parameter-enumeration oracle. My own pre-registered closure condition is now met — the recognized set matches the published contract on every route, across three per-operation negative controls and ten candidate filters, and every disclosed value (`expected number <= 50` on `/v2/rooms`, `expected number <= 500` on `/v2/guest-links`, `required query parameter is missing` on `attendances.to`, the `department/room/meeting` enum, the Go time layout `2006-01-02T15:04:05.999999999Z07:00`, the RFC-5322 email check) equals what `/v2/docs/openapi.json` already publishes. The oracle reproduces the public specification and nothing beyond it. Downgraded to informational; its real value is that it is the instrument which converted H1's scope half from a documentation reading into a measured server-side negative.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ apis.alfaview.com: the two-tier 401 on `/v2/guest-links` and its misleading base64 message. No header → 401/107B md5 `60ed2f29`; any `Authorization: Bearer …` → 401/122B md5 `3d0dc74b`. The second tier's text claims a base64 failure it never tests: `Zm9vOmJhcg==` is valid base64 and still receives `"No base64 encoded access token was provided"`, and so does a raw UUID. The differential is keyed on header presence, which the caller already knows, so it carries no attacker value, and the program rejects descriptive errors. Its one real consequence is the control it supplies: because a well-formed-but-invalid credential and a malformed one collapse to the same tier, there is no third tier and therefore **no pre-auth token-validity oracle** on the list routes.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ apis.alfaview.com — my own prior-cycle generalization, retracted and narrowed: "the whole authenticated surface returns one uniform 401/107B regardless of path validity or query parameters" was over-fitted to the no-header case. This cycle found a second, md5-distinct 401 tier that every request carrying an `Authorization` header receives. The correct statement is that the surface is uniform *per header-presence state*, and the md5 is the reliable discriminator — the byte lengths I recorded across cycles (107/115/122) are not stable and should not be used as evidence of sameness.

[LEARN] NO_DELTA @ apis/sso/app/tools: OpenAPI md5 `357b94d367909a40b9299b543d23712b` (127532B, 37 paths, `components.securitySchemes` and top-level `security` both absent) for the 35th consecutive cycle; sso OIDC 200/2169B and JWKS 200/16257B size-stable; `/v2/auth/api-key` OPTIONS 405 `allow: POST`; `tools.alfaview.com/whiteboard/` 404/47B gRPC status envelope; `app.alfaview.com` bundle unrotated at 1092529B under the same content-hashed filename, confirmed by a single HEAD rather than a 1MB re-download.

## STEP 7 — RISK

[RISK] alfaview: 73 — up 1 from 72, and the smallness of that number is the honest reading. This cycle produced no exposure: 37 requests, all GET, HEAD or OPTIONS except the one `POST` proposed as next action and not yet fired, no credential used or sought, no account created, no mutating verb, no response body read for content beyond validator field names, and no guest, user, room or tenant data touched. What moved is evidentiary, not expository. The un-narrowability of the credential-bearing list is no longer an inference from documentation but a complete measurement across all four list routes, and the instrument that made it complete also **closed itself**: the enumerator reproduces the published contract exactly, so the oracle I was carrying at 78 is demoted to informational by the closure criterion I had written down in advance. That is a net loss of one finding and a net gain of rigor on another, and the two roughly cancel. The offsets are real. Negative: the authenticated surface remains hygienic — one 401 per header-presence state, no reflection beyond `query.<name>` and the Go time layout, no vendor headers, no divergence between malformed and well-formed input on any path, and this cycle's entire budget bought no data. Positive: the highest-severity chain in the program now carries two independent measured halves (un-narrowability complete, payload credential-bearing at 10x page size) whose only missing element is a single self-issued token, and the second-highest (`api-key`) has a declared 403 tier that no one has ever exercised. The discipline that matters most this cycle is the one I had to apply to myself twice: retracting a "uniform 401" claim that a two-tier differential falsified, and demoting a finding at 78 because my own stated success criterion for closing it had been met. Both were found because I ran the control that last cycle's positive-control omission had made unnecessary — the same methodological error, once corrected, then paid out a second time in the opposite direction. Introspection at 94 remains the highest-confidence weakness; the honest read of this program today is that the decisive step on every HIGH-value chain is one email-verified self-owned account, and that step is not mine to take.
