- **[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ staging-support.alfaview.com / staging-internal.alfaview.com / staging-alfatraining.alfaview.com:** three never-probed hosts closed on first contact — nginx `401 www-authenticate: Basic realm="Restricted Area"` on `/en/`, `/wp-json/`, `/readme.html`, `/wp-login.php` (with `/xmlrpc.php` 403 as an edge rule), plus two 401 edge-proxy hosts. Public login panel and default-credential testing are rejected classes, and the production sibling of the WordPress host remains fully mapped and closed with no unauthenticated exposure. Five of the 26 newly-discovered crt.sh names are now inventoried and closed; the remaining 17 are `000`.
- **[LEARN] REJECTED METHOD-BUDGET ERROR @ my own last-cycle [NEXT]:** the proposed `POST /v2/auth/api-key` was not executable under the `GET/HEAD/OPTIONS only` rule, and the hypothesis carrying it was tagged `testability: PASSIVE` when its only decisive step is a POST. Re-tagged. Recording it because a NEXT action that violates the stated method budget is a planning error, not a finding, and the tag error would have made an unmeasured endpoint look measured.
- **[LEARN] NO_DELTA @ apis/sso/app/tools:** OpenAPI `357b94d367909a40b9299b543d23712b` (127532B/37 paths), OIDC `f78a08fced8b042093396383b74d8458` (2169B), JWKS `3f8d456c7fd92c6107f3fd77732bd0f4` (16257B), `/v2/users/me` 401/107B md5 `60ed2f29` — 36th consecutive byte-stable cycle, no rotation, no regression.

## STEP 7 — RISK

[RISK] alfaview: 74 — up 1 from 73, and the movement is small on purpose. This cycle produced no exposure: roughly 60 requests, every one of them a GET at 1 rps, no credential used or sought, no account created, no mutating verb, no header that could alter the auth state, no response body read beyond the three 19-byte router envelopes and the four named validator field names already in the record. No guest, user, room or tenant data touched, and the new hosts found were all closed on contact without a single 200 carrying business data. Two things genuinely moved. Positive: the program now has a **measured, pre-auth, credential-free instrument for enumerating the API's route and method tables**, proven with four negative and two positive controls and three stable body md5s, and it has already produced one concrete artifact of exactly the class it is good for — the undeclared `DELETE` on the undocumented `/v2/users/invitations`. That is a capability the program did not have, and it is the cheapest possible one to abuse later, which is why I am scoring it as an enabler rather than a non-finding. Negative, and I will not bury it: I filed a second retraction of my own claim in two consecutive cycles, and the cause was identical both times — generalising a security control from a probe set that contained no negative control for the branch being described. Last cycle it was a missing positive control on a recognized parameter; this cycle it was never having probed an unregistered path at all, which means every "35+ consecutive cycles, no new endpoints" note in this program's history, including my own, was a statement about the routes I happened to choose rather than about the namespace. That is a real and repeated failure of method, and it costs more than the finding it accompanies, because a surface described as uniform is a surface nobody tests. The offsets: introspection at 94 remains the highest-confidence weakness and is now corroborated by a same-build control; the guest-link IDOR at 80 carries two independent measured halves and is blocked on exactly one step; and the 20-route sweep, though it returned 19 negative results, is what now licenses the claim that no debug, test, admin or metrics route exists under `/v2` — a claim that was previously asserted rather than tested. The honest read of this program today is unchanged: the decisive step on every HIGH-value chain is one email-verified self-owned account, and that step is not mine to take.
