899:[HYP] Client authentication on the RFC 7662 introspection endpoint is not enforced on any channel, and the same build enforces token validation correctly elsewhere
900-class: AUTH
901-asset: sso.alfaview.com — `POST /oauth2/introspect`
902-confidence: 94
903-reasoning: Standing finding, 30+ consecutive stable cycles, not re-probed this cycle (metadata re-probed and byte-stable at 200/2169B, JWKS 200/16257B). Fabricated `client_id` is accepted on both the POST-body and the HTTP Basic channel, returning `200 {"active":false}`; the only residual check is a Basic-vs-body `client_id_mismatch` (401). Discovery advertises `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported` including `client_secret_basic`, a secret-bearing method never verified at runtime, and `introspection_endpoint` is absent from discovery while the endpoint stays live (`OPTIONS` 405) — advertisement is not a reliable liveness signal. The same-deployment control is the load-bearing part: `/oauth2/userinfo` in the same FusionAuth 1.63.0 build tiers correctly, rejects `alg=none`, HS256 keyed with the published 4096-bit RSA public key, HS256 with a random key, and RS256 self-signed, and JOSE-shaped self-issued tokens across both candidate issuers, all with byte-identical `access_token_failed_processing` versus a distinct `access_token_malformed` for non-JOSE input. So the introspection gap is a missing check, not an architectural choice, which is why confidence sits at 94 rather than 92.
904-evidence_needed: A valid token submitted with a fabricated `client_id` to show what the unenforced check would have protected. That requires an account and is therefore outside this cycle's reach; the control comparison is already complete.
905-verify_steps: Passive: `GET https://sso.alfaview.com/.well-known/openid-configuration` and `GET https://sso.alfaview.com/.well-known/jwks.json`, recording sizes and md5 — both stable this cycle at 2169B and 16257B. Authenticated, read-only: `POST /oauth2/introspect` with `token=<self-issued token>&client_id=<fabricated>` and record whether the response returns `active` claims it should not disclose. No token other than one the tester owns.
906-impact: Any party can ask the IdP whether an arbitrary token is live and receive back its full claim set, so token validity and contents are confirmable without client credentials. This does not by itself yield a token, and it is capped by the program's own note on authentication data; the reportable core is the missing client authentication on a token-introspection endpoint, not token theft. HIGH severity for a production IdP, zero impact without a token to introspect.
907-testability: AUTH_HELPED
