[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: the participant-identity read is the program's first *self-serving* IDOR oracle. Every prior chain in this program has been blocked on a missing input — a token, an account, a user ID. This endpoint hands out the user ID. `GET /v2/rooms/{id}/participants` returns, for every principal currently connected to a room and bucketed by `subRoomId`, the internal `userId` UUID plus `displayName`, `isGuest`, `isDialIn`, `onBreak`, `inWaitingRoom` and `isSpectator` — and those UUIDs are the sole input to `DELETE /v2/users/{id}` and `PATCH /v2/rooms/{roomId}/permissions/{userId}`, the two unscoped destructive operations this program has carried as its standing IDOR target with no way to supply an identifier. The vendor documents the gate for exactly this data and nowhere else: `TokenUserPermissions.userShow` is "If true, the user can see the user names of all users in rooms they can access", in a closed 7-boolean all-required vocabulary whose other six fields are about company/room/user administration and bear no relation to participant identity. Exhaustive reference search confirms **no operation in the 57-operation contract references `TokenUserPermissions` except `GetTokenInfo` returning it** — so the enforcement point is undocumented and this operation is the only plausible consumer. Three independent corroborations that alfaview considers participant identity sensitive: `RoomFeatures` ships `allowCopyRoomInfo` ("Allows moderators to copy the contents of the participant list to the clipboard"), `showRaisedHandsOnlyToModerators` and `shareContentOnlyToModerators`, and `examScreenshare` shares every participant's monitors with moderators. The room-level `Permissions` vocabulary is a closed 9-boolean set with **no** participant-visibility axis, so there is no in-room way to withhold list access; `userShow` is the only expressible gate. The severity hinge is token class: `POST /v2/auth/guest-link` is unauthenticated, takes only `{companyId, roomId, accessKey}`, and returns a real `accessToken` whenever the link is valid (the spec marks it "Can be empty if the guest link is not yet / no longer valid" — empty only when invalid), so an invitation URL handed to an external third party is a plausible route to employees' and moderators' internal UUIDs with no company account involved. Confidence 78, `AUTH_HELPED`, **zero wire evidence of a defect claimed** — the contract shape and the single documented gate are measured; the authorization behaviour is unmeasured.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com — methodological, and it reverses a conclusion I reached mid-cycle: I first read the missing-403 audit as evidence that the participant read was *ungated*, then inverted it. The 403 split across 9 room-scoped GETs is 4 with (participants, attendances, group-links, guest-links) and 5 without (room, features, language-settings, permissions, subrooms) — so 403-declaration draws a line around credential, attendance and identity reads and deliberately not around room configuration. The defensible claim is therefore **fit, not uniqueness**: `userShow` is the only permission in the contract whose documented meaning is this endpoint's payload, and `token-info` makes the test a binary read of the caller's own bit rather than an inference. I am recording the reversal because the specific error — generalising a control from an absence in a declaration table rather than from a behavioural measurement — is the same class of error I retracted in each of the last two cycles, and it would have produced an overstated finding in the same direction both times.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (negative result) @ apis.alfaview.com: the contract's structural change produced **zero** new pre-authentication exposure. All 11 newly-surfaced paths return 401/107B md5 `60ed2f29` — `/v2/users/me/company` and the 10 room-scoped reads including the never-before-probed `participants`, `features`, `file-share-limits`, `file-share-settings`, `language-settings`, `passcode` and `subrooms` — and malformed identifiers (`not-a-uuid`) still return 401 on both a declared GET and a declared 401-only route, so the UUID path validator still runs behind authentication. The 27-GET sweep left the pre-auth validator map unchanged at 9 operations, of which only `/v2/stats` (422/319B md5 6d812e83) and `/v2/rooms/{roomId}/attendances` (422/232B md5 b4adcd15) disclose per-field names. The new schemas are individually unremarkable for attack surface: `RoomSettingsUpdate` is `additionalProperties:false` with only `defaultAccessLevel`, `enabled` and `participantsCanUpload`, closing mass assignment on that route; `RoomLimits` is read-only quota disclosure; `Passcode` returns `passcode` plus `required` and is 401-gated; `RoomFeaturesEdit` is `additionalProperties:false` with 14 nullable tri-state fields and no non-boolean injection surface; the subroom family is correctly room-scoped on both PATCH and DELETE, and `subrooms/{subroomId}` confirms `allow: DELETE, PATCH` exactly as declared.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (negative result, and it downgrades a standing hypothesis) @ apis.alfaview.com: the unscoped-resource-update defect is **not** a platform pattern. I tested the six newly-surfaced schema families for unscoped siblings — the exact shape already confirmed on `/v2/guest-links/{id}` and `/v2/group-links/{id}` — with 9 HEAD probes: `/v2/subrooms`, `/v2/subrooms/{id}`, `/v2/permissions/{id}`, `/v2/room-features`, `/v2/passcode`, `/v2/attendances`, `/v2/participants`, `/v2/file-share-settings`, `/v2/room-permissions`. All nine returned 404/19B. Combined with the `Allow:`-versus-spec check matching the contract exactly on all 11 new paths, the undocumented `DELETE` on `/v2/users/invitations` now looks like a single instance rather than the first of many, which is the pre-registered upgrade condition for the 63 route-enumeration finding and was not met. The 405 branch is therefore parked, and I am explicitly declining to spend further cycles extending a reconnaissance primitive when three HIGH chains are blocked on one absent account.
[LEARN] NO_DELTA @ sso/app/tools: sso.alfaview.com metadata byte-stable for a 28th consecutive cycle (OIDC 200/2169B md5 `f78a08fc`, JWKS 200/16257B md5 `3f8d456c`, 7 RSA keys, `introspection_endpoint` still absent from discovery while `/oauth2/introspect` stays live at OPTIONS 405). apis.alfaview.com OpenAPI md5 moved for the first time in the program's recorded history, to `284a3383c1ac3cfc9152ffcc631891f2` (132100B, 38 paths, 57 operations), byte-identical on beta-apis.alfaview.com, so the same code path serves both environments. I could not identify *which* path the count delta added, and I am not claiming `/v2/room-types` as new since it is already recorded in `leads/lead-bigpickle.md` — an honest null result on the identity of the delta, as distinct from the measured result on its size.
[RISK] alfaview: 75 — up 1 from 74, and the move is small because this cycle found a chain, not a compromise. Roughly 59 requests, every one a GET or HEAD at 1 rps, zero credentials used or sought, zero accounts created, no mutating verb, no header capable of altering auth state, and no response body read beyond three 19-byte router envelopes, four declared schema field lists, and status/length/md5 triples. No guest, user, room or tenant data touched, and nothing returned 200 with business data. What genuinely improved: the program now has its first self-serving IDOR oracle — a GET that supplies the `userId` the standing destructive-operation chain has lacked for 36 cycles — paired with a named vendor-declared gate (`userShow`) and a self-service oracle for it (`token-info`), which turns the next cycle's decisive test from an informed guess into a two-request binary read. That is a real reduction in the cost of the program's most important pending step, and it is why the score moved at all. What I will not bury: I filed a third methodological retraction in four consecutive cycles, and the cause is the same each time — generalising a security control from a probe set that lacked a negative control for the branch being described. This cycle I inferred "ungated" from a missing 403 in a declaration table, and the measured split (4 of 9 room GETs declare 403) shows the declaration table is not a proxy for behaviour. Three of four cycles now ending in a self-correction is a poor rate, and it is the real cost of this cycle, larger than the finding it accompanies. Two further limits stated plainly: the contract delta is measured in size but not identified in identity, so I have a 38-path count and no new path name; and the new IDOR hypothesis has **zero** wire evidence — I am not claiming the authorization check is missing, only that the vendor ships a permission for it, that exactly one operation could enforce it, and that this operation returns the identifier the program'