## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

`[HYP]` A single unscoped, credential-bearing guest-link read returns the complete unauthenticated redemption tuple in one response
class: IDOR
asset: apis.alfaview.com — `GET /v2/guest-links/{id}` (GetGuestLink); sibling `GET /v2/group-links/{id}` (GetGroupLink); IDs from `GET /v2/guest-links`
confidence: 74
reasoning: The 38-path contract declares `GET` on `/v2/guest-links/{id}` with exactly two parameters — `Authorization` (header, required:false) and `id` (path, required:true). No `companyId`, no `roomId`, no paging. Declares 200/401/403/404/422, the 403 being "The requesting user does not have the necessary permissions." The 200 schema is `GuestLink`, `additionalProperties:false`, 12 of 15 fields required: `id`, `roomId`, `createdBy`, `createdAt`, `permissionGroupId`, `accessKey`, `emailAddress`, `locale`, `origin`, `dialInAllowed`, `dialInCode`, `joinUrl`. Redemption is `POST /v2/auth/guest-link` = `AuthenticateGuestLinkRequestBody`, `additionalProperties:false`, required `['companyId','roomId','accessKey']`, unauthenticated, whose `accessToken` is documented empty only when the link is invalid. **A single 200 therefore returns two of the three redemption fields (`accessKey`, `roomId`) in one response**, and the caller supplies the third (its own `companyId`). `GroupLink` is identical with `description`/`joinLink`/`joinUrl`/`accessKey`/`dialInCode` required. This is a strictly narrower-addressing sibling of the standing list (80): the list needs paging and yields 500 records; this needs one UUID and yields exactly the object. Counter-evidence stated plainly: the vendor *declares* the 403 tier, meaning an authorization check is anticipated here.
evidence_needed: One self-issued token and one guest-link ID the tester owns. `GET /v2/auth/token-info` recording `permissions` verbatim; `GET /v2/guest-links/<own-id>` recording status and content-length only. A 200 returning `accessKey`+`roomId` under a token whose `TokenUserPermissions` holds no guest-link axis is a confirmed IDOR; a 403 closes it.
verify_steps: AUTH_HELPED, read-only, one account, no third-party tenant. (1) `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/auth/token-info` with `Authorization: Bearer <self-issued token>`, record `permissions` verbatim. (2) `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/users/me/company` — required, because `TokenInfo` carries no company scope. (3) `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/guest-links/<own-guest-link-id>`, record status + content-length only; if 200, record field *names* present, never values. (4) Repeat for a link in a room the token can access but does not administer. **Do not run the redemption step** — POSTing a harvested `accessKey` to `/v2/auth/guest-link` converts a read test into a credential-use test and belongs to a separate authorization review.
impact: If the 403 does not fire, one low-privilege token converts one enumeration into a point read of a live room-entry credential: `accessKey` (redeemable for a real room `accessToken` via the unauthenticated `POST /v2/auth/guest-link`), `joinUrl`, `dialInCode` (telephony PIN, required unconditionally beside `dialInAllowed`), and the guest's `emailAddress`. Unlike the list route it needs no paging, no `limit` tuning and no 500-record harvest — the cheapest possible redemption of the company's issued invitations, and identifiers are supplied company-wide by the un-narrowable list. HIGH if a low-privilege or guest-scoped token gets 200 outside its room; MEDIUM if only that room's admins may read it, which would be intended. Unproven on the wire — zero credentialed requests sent, no authorization behaviour claimed.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

`[HYP]` The two field-identical permission vocabularies carry contradictory documented semantics, so a representable privilege state exists that the enforcement layer has no documented rule to resolve
class: BUSLOGIC
asset: apis.alfaview.com — `InviteUserPermissions` (body of `POST /v2/users/invitation`) vs `TokenUserPermissions` (200 of `GET /v2/auth/token-info`)
confidence: 62
reasoning: Verified programmatically. Both schemas have **exactly identical field sets** — 7 booleans, all required, `additionalProperties:false` — {manageCompany, roomAdmin, roomCreate, roomList, userAdmin, userList, userShow}. Descriptions diverge: `InviteUserPermissions` documents three implications; `TokenUserPermissions` documents **none** (zero "Implies" occurrences). `InviteUserPermissions` is referenced exactly once in the whole contract, as `UserInvitation.permissions`; `TokenUserPermissions` exactly once, as `TokenInfo.permissions` — and is **never** accepted as a request body, so no API exposes writing a token's permission set. The platform can therefore *express* `{manageCompany: true, userShow: false}` at invitation time (the implication is prose, not a schema constraint) and *observe* it via token-info, with the enforcement layer's interpretation undocumented. This also sharpens the standing `userShow` hypothesis: `userShow` is the only field whose documented meaning is participant-identity disclosure, and it is one of the three `manageCompany` is documented to imply — so the two documented resolution rules disagree about whether it is independently controllable. That disagreement is exactly what a per-field gate on `GET /v2/rooms/{id}/participants` must adjudicate.
evidence_needed: A token issued with `userShow:false` while a company-level bit is true, plus a room that token can access. Creating that state requires a mutating invitation call — out of my reach, and it is the one place in the program where the blocker is a *crafted* token, not merely an account.
verify_steps: HUMAN_ONLY for the decisive step; passive portion complete. Read-only: `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/docs/openapi.json`, diff the `description` strings of `components.schemas.InviteUserPermissions` and `TokenUserPermissions`, confirm zero "Implies" in the latter (done). Decisive, for a human with a test tenant: invite via `POST /v2/users/invitation` with all 7 booleans and `{"manageCompany": true, "userShow": false}`; as that user call `GET /v2/auth/token-info` recording `permissions` verbatim, then `GET /v2/rooms/<own-room-id>/participants` recording status only. A 200 with `userShow:false` confirms the divergence is behaviourally live. Count only, never read identity values.
impact: If enforcement resolves implications from `TokenUserPermissions` (which documents none) rather than `InviteUserPermissions` (which documents three), a user the invitation layer treats as a full company manager may be denied participant-identity reads — or, damagingly, a narrowly-granted user may inherit the participant list through an implication applied opposite to the one the invitation schema documents. The asset at risk is `GET /v2/rooms/{id}/participants`, whose payload is the internal `userId` UUID that `DELETE /v2/users/{id}` and `PATCH /v2/rooms/{roomId}/permissions/{userId}` consume as their sole input. MEDIUM and honestly speculative: this is a documentation-semantics divergence, not a demonstrated defect, and it is entirely plausible the server treats the 7 booleans as independent, in which case nothing is wrong.
testability: HUMAN_ONLY

`[HYP]` Client authentication on the RFC 7662 introspection endpoint is not enforced on any channel, while the same build enforces token validation correctly elsewhere
class: AUTH
asset: sso.alfaview.com — `POST /oauth2/introspect`
confidence: 94
reasoning: Standing, 32nd consecutive cycle, not re-probed. Metadata byte-stable this cycle: OIDC 200/2169B md5 `f78a08fc` (issuer `acme.com`, `introspection_endpoint` absent while the endpoint is live at `OPTIONS` 405), JWKS 200/16257B md5 `3f8d456c`, 7 RSA keys. Fabricated `client_id` accepted on both POST-body and HTTP Basic, returning `200 {"active":false}`; only residual check is Basic-vs-body `client_id_mismatch` (401). Discovery advertises `client_secret_basic`, a secret-bearing method never verified at runtime. The load-bearing control is `/oauth2/userinfo` in the same FusionAuth 1.63.0 build: it tiers correctly and rejects `alg=none`, HS256 keyed with the published 4096-bit RSA public key, HS256 with a random key, and RS256 self-signed, all byte-identical `access_token_failed_processing`, versus a distinct `access_token_malformed` for non-JOSE input. The gap is a missing check, not an architectural choice.
evidence_needed: A valid token submitted with a fabricated `client_id` to show what the unenforced check would have protected. Requires an account. The control comparison is complete.
verify_steps: Passive (done this cycle): `GET https://sso.alfaview.com/.well-known/openid-configuration` and `GET https://sso.alfaview.com/.well-known/jwks.json`, record size and md5. Authenticated, read-only: `POST https://sso.alfaview.com/oauth2/introspect`, `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, body `token=<self-issued token>&client_id=<fabricated>`, recording whether `active` claims are disclosed. No token other than one the tester owns.
impact: Any party can ask the IdP whether an arbitrary token is live and receive its full claim set, so token validity and contents are confirmable without client credentials. Does not by itself yield a token and is capped by the program's own note on authentication data; the reportable core is the missing client authentication on a token-introspection endpoint, not token theft. HIGH for a production IdP, zero impact without a token to introspect.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

