## STEP 6 — LEARNING

`[LEARN]` ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: the contract declares `GET /v2/guest-links/{id}` and `GET /v2/group-links/{id}` — single-object reads addressed by bare UUID with **no** `companyId`, `roomId`, `pageToken` or `limit` — returning `GuestLink`/`GroupLink` with `accessKey`, `dialInCode`, `joinUrl`, guest `emailAddress` and `roomId` required. Because `POST /v2/auth/guest-link` redeems on `{companyId, roomId, accessKey}` and is unauthenticated, one 200 here hands back **two of the three** redemption fields in a single response. This is the practical successor to the list-based finding: no paging, no `limit` tuning, no 500-record harvest. Counter-evidence preserved: the operation *declares* a 403 tier. Unproven on the wire.

`[LEARN]` ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (negative, class-closing) @ apis.alfaview.com: mass assignment is closed platform-wide. Every one of the 21 distinct request-body schemas reachable across all 57 operations is `additionalProperties: false` — including `GuestLinkUpdate`, `PermissionsEdit`, `RoomCreate`/`RoomUpdate`, `MeetingCreate`/`MeetingUpdate` and `UserInvitation`. Notably the platform constrains update schemas where it chose to (`UserUpdate` is 7 cosmetic fields with no `id`, `emailAddress` or `permissions`), which is what makes the guest-link `PATCH` shape notable: it is closed, but it is closed around `emailAddress` + `permissionGroupId` + `validFrom`/`validUntil` + `sendEmail`, i.e. credential rebinding by design rather than injection.

`[LEARN]` ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (negative) @ apis.alfaview.com: the pre-auth method-resolution oracle is reliable, and this cycle it was established with **positive controls** rather than assumed. App-runtime POST-only routes emit `405 allow: POST` (`/v2/auth/guest-link`, `/v2/meetings/{id}/cancellation`), the Go-runtime `/v2/users/invitation` emits `405 allow: DELETE, POST`, and the DELETE-only `/v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links/{id}` emits `405 allow: DELETE` — all before any 401.

`[LEARN]` ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (negative) @ apis.alfaview.com: `/v2/room-types` probed for the first time in 38 cycles returns `401/107B`, byte-identical to the standing probe. The 38th path in the contract adds zero pre-authentication exposure, closing the last never-probed path.

`[LEARN]` ACCEPTED OTHER (contract semantics) @ apis.alfaview.com: `InviteUserPermissions` and `TokenUserPermissions` are field-identical 7-boolean closed vocabularies with **divergent documented semantics** — the former documents three implications, the latter none. `TokenUserPermissions` is referenced exactly once in the whole contract, as a response, and is never accepted as a request body, so no API exposes writing a token's permission set. `userShow` is documented as implied by `manageCompany` in one vocabulary and stands alone in the other, which is precisely the ambiguity a per-field gate on `GET /v2/rooms/{id}/participants` must resolve.

`[LEARN]` REJECTED (self) @ apis.alfaview.com: I asserted mid-cycle that `GET /v2/guest-links/{id}` and `GET /v2/group-links/{id}` were **undeclared hidden reads**, on the strength of a `401`-instead-of-`405` pattern against POST-only controls. The controls plus the spec killed it: both paths declare `GET` in the current contract, and the 401 is the correct auth gate on a declared GET. The 401-vs-405 pattern was never evidence of a hidden route at all. This is the fourth consecutive cycle I have generalised a control from a probe set lacking the right control, and the cause was identical to the previous three: I had a pattern and no positive control for the *absence* branch. It is recorded rather than quietly dropped.

## STEP 7 — RISK

`[RISK]` alfaview: 76 — up 1 from 75, and the move is small because this cycle found declared surface, not a compromise. Roughly 20 requests, every one a GET or HEAD, ≤1 rps, zero credentials used or sought, zero accounts created, no mutating verb, no header capable of altering auth state, and no response body read beyond three tiny envelopes (16B/47B and a 19B router string) plus status/length/md5 triples. Nothing returned 200 carrying business data; the only 200s were the two public documents I was measuring. What genuinely improved: the program now has its shortest chain to a live credential — a contract-declared, unscoped, single-object GET on a guest link that returns `accessKey` and `roomId` together, i.e. two of the three fields the unauthenticated redemption endpoint needs, with the caller supplying only its own `companyId`. That is a two-request version of a chain previously carried as a 500-record-per-page harvest, and it is the first finding in this program whose *complete* exploit path is expressible in the published contract rather than requiring an identifier from a second enumeration. Mass assignment also closed for the whole REST surface on evidence rather than on spot checks.

What I will not bury: I filed a **fourth** methodological retraction in five consecutive cycles, and the mechanism is now identical each time — generalising a security control from a probe set that lacked a control for the branch being described. This cycle I read a `401`-vs-`405` pattern as proof of undeclared surface, when the two controls I eventually ran plus the spec showed the pattern was just the auth gate on a declared route. Four of five cycles ending in self-correction is a poor rate and it is the real cost of this cycle, larger than the finding it accompanies. Two further limits stated plainly: the `+1` path delta between the 37-path and 38-path contracts remains **unidentified** — I found a method-level delta on two existing paths, which is a different kind of change and does not account for the path count, and I am not claiming `/v2/room-types` as the cause without evidence. And the top new finding has **zero** wire evidence: I am claiming the contract declares an unscoped credential-bearing read, not that any authorization check is missing — the declared 403 is real counter-evidence and I have weighted it.

The honest read of this program is unchanged in substance and cheaper in cost. Four HIGH chains — introspect at 94, the guest-link list at 80, the `userShow` participants gate at 78, and now the guest-link point read at 74 — collapse onto one input: a single email-verified self-owned account. Every one of the four has its contract half fully measured and its authorization behaviour entirely unmeasured, because every request stops at the token gate. That step is not mine to take.
