## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

`[HYP]` A permission requirement is declared for reading guest-link credentials company-wide but not for reading group-link credentials, from otherwise identical operations
class: AUTHZ
asset: apis.alfaview.com — `GET /v2/group-links` (`ListGroupLinks`) vs `GET /v2/guest-links` (`ListGuestLinks`)
confidence: 70
reasoning: A controlled pair, which is the strongest evidence class available without a token. Both operations declare parameters `Authorization` (header), `pageToken` (query), `limit` (query). Both 200s return a `PaginatedBodyList*` wrapper. `GuestLink` requires `accessKey`, `joinUrl`, `dialInCode`, `roomId`, `id`; `GroupLink` requires `accessKey`, `joinUrl`, `dialInCode`, `roomId`, `id`, plus `description` and `joinLink`. `ListGuestLinks` declares 200/401/403 with the 403 documented "The requesting user does not have the necessary permissions." `ListGroupLinks` declares **200/401 only**. The load-bearing point is the *asymmetry*, not the absence: the contract is otherwise identical between these two operations, so uniform spec sloppiness would not plausibly omit 403 from exactly one half of a twin pair. The base rate I measured cuts against overreading — 14 of 27 GETs declare no 403 — so the finding rests entirely on the pair, not on the omission. Both halves carry the same credential class, and `accessKey` is redeemable against the unauthenticated `POST /v2/auth/guest-link` on `{companyId, roomId, accessKey}`.
evidence_needed: One self-issued token holding the lowest available privilege, plus its own `companyId`. `GET /v2/guest-links` and `GET /v2/group-links` with the *same* token, recording status only. A 403 on guest links and 200 on group links is a confirmed missing authorization check; 403 on both means the omission is a spec defect and the control is real.
verify_steps: AUTH_HELPED, read-only, one account, own tenant only. (1) `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/auth/token-info`, record `permissions` verbatim. (2) `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/users/me/company` for `companyId` (absent from `TokenInfo`). (3) `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/guest-links` — record status and content-length only. (4) `GET https://apis.alfaview.com/v2/group-links` — identical recording. Never read a body; a 200 payload is the tenant's own link credentials and the program excludes exposure of authentication data.
impact: If enforcement is symmetric across these twin handlers, the guest-link 403 that the standing 80 finding treats as its obstacle is real, and group links are the ungated half of the identical credential class — company-wide `accessKey`, `joinUrl`, `dialInCode` and `roomId` for every group link in the tenant, from one paged call. Each `accessKey` is redeemable for a live room `accessToken` through the unauthenticated `POST /v2/auth/guest-link`. The effect on the 80 finding is a reframe rather than an addition: guest links are gated, group links may not be, and the aggregate credential harvest is the union of both. HIGH if a non-admin token gets 200; NOT APPLICABLE if both 403.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

`[HYP]` A room's full permission table is readable without a declared permission gate, and the same resource's create operation can mint an admin record for a caller-supplied principal
class: IDOR
asset: apis.alfaview.com — `GET`/`POST /v2/rooms/{roomId}/permissions` (`ListPermissions` / `CreatePermissions`); `participantId` polymorphism
confidence: 66
reasoning: `ListPermissions` declares 200/401/404/422 — **no 403** — and returns `RoomPermissions`, whose `userPermissions[]`, `guestLinkPermissions[]` and `groupLinkPermissions[]` each carry a `ParticipantPermissions.participantId` and a `Permissions` object. All three writes on the same resource (`CreatePermissions`, `UpdatePermissions`, `DeletePermissions`) *do* declare 403. That within-resource asymmetry is the evidence; the bare absence is not, given the 14/27 base rate. The create path is the second half: `CreatePermissions` takes `ParticipantPermissions` with `participantId` documented as "The user ID / guest link ID / group link ID", and its `permissions` is `Permissions` with **all nine booleans required, `admin` among them** — so a create can set `admin: true` explicitly. The create's documented failure modes are 400 (bad body), 401, 403, 404 "Room was not found", 409 "The resource already exists", 422. Its 404 names **only the room**, while `UpdatePermissions`' 404 names "Room **or user permission** was not found" — so the contract asserts a permission-entry existence lookup on the update path and asserts no equivalent on the create path beyond duplicate detection. 422's "a precondition was not met" is generic enough to cover a membership check, so I cannot exclude validation. Note `promote` is documented as permission to promote permissions "for other users", which suggests granting a record to *oneself* may sit below the bar `promote` gates — the reason the self-grant variant matters more than the cross-user one.
evidence_needed: One self-issued token with any permission in one self-owned room, plus one foreign `participantId`. A 204 on `POST` with `admin: true` for a principal not present in that room is a confirmed IDOR; a 422 with a membership-shaped error is a confirmed control and closes it.
verify_steps: AUTH_HELPED, read-only unless explicitly escalated; `POST` is a write and therefore **not** mine to send. For a human tester with a test tenant: (1) `GET /v2/auth/token-info`, record `permissions` verbatim. (2) `GET /v2/rooms` for own room IDs. (3) `GET /v2/rooms/{own-room}/permissions`, record status and whether `userPermissions`/`guestLinkPermissions`/`groupLinkPermissions` are populated, never values. (4) The decisive `POST` is a write on a live tenant and needs explicit program sign-off plus a sandbox. Read-only stage 3 alone is a meaningful finding if it returns 200 with populated `guestLinkPermissions` for a token whose `TokenUserPermissions` holds no admin or roomAdmin bit.
impact: If the read is ungated, any authenticated principal can enumerate the internal `userId` UUIDs and guest-link and group-link IDs of any room it can name — and those `userId` UUIDs are the sole input to `DELETE /v2/users/{id}` and `PATCH /v2/rooms/{roomId}/permissions/{userId}`. If the create is unvalidated, one low-privilege principal can mint an `admin: true` record for itself, escalating to the room administrator described as able to "edit or delete rooms, change room settings or features". Both variants are **intra-tenant and need one account**, not two — the cross-tenant framing that made me park this last turn was wrong about the primitive. HIGH if the self-grant succeeds; MEDIUM if the create is gated on a bit any participant holds.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

`[HYP]` Client authentication on the RFC 7662 introspection endpoint is not enforced on any channel
class: AUTH
asset: sso.alfaview.com — `POST /oauth2/introspect`
confidence: 94
reasoning: Standing, 33rd cycle, not re-probed. OIDC byte-stable at 200/2169B md5 `f78a08fc` (issuer `acme.com`, `introspection_endpoint` absent while the endpoint is live), JWKS 200/16257B md5 `3f8d456c`, 7 RSA keys. Fabricated `client_id` accepted on both POST-body and Basic, returning `200 {"active":false}`; residual check is a Basic-vs-body `client_id_mismatch` (401). Discovery advertises `client_secret_basic`, never verified at runtime. The control is `/oauth2/userinfo` in the same FusionAuth 1.63.0 build, which tiers correctly against `alg=none`, HS256 with the published RSA public key, HS256 random, and RS256 self-signed. Missing check, not architectural choice.
evidence_needed: A valid token submitted with a fabricated `client_id`. Needs an account. Controls complete.
verify_steps: Passive done. Authenticated read-only: `POST https://sso.alfaview.com/oauth2/introspect`, `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, `token=<self-issued>&client_id=<fabricated>`, recording whether `active` claims are disclosed.
impact: Any party can determine whether an arbitrary token is live and read its claims, without client credentials. Does not yield a token; capped by the program's authentication-data exclusion. HIGH for a production IdP, zero impact without a token.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

## STEP 4 — SELF-CRITIQUE

Three retractions this turn, and one of them reversed a decision I made an hour ago.

`[PARKED→UNPARKED]` I parked the `participantId` hypothesis citing a "409 that its PATCH sibling does not", reading the 409 as evidence of participant↔room binding validation. Both halves were wrong. The 409 is documented **"The resource already exists"** — a duplicate-create guard, not a binding check. And there is no PATCH on the collection path at all; `UpdatePermissions` is on `/v2/rooms/{roomId}/permissions/{userId}`, a different resource, so my comparison was between two different things. I had inferred semantics from a status number instead of reading the description sitting next to it in the file I had already downloaded. The hypothesis is now filed at 66, and its primitive is **intra-tenant**, not cross-tenant — the "needs a second tenant" reasoning that completed the parking was also wrong.

`[PARKED]` The bare absence of a `403` is not evidence. I built the `ListPermissions` finding on the missing 403, then measured the base rate as a control and found 14 of 27 GETs omit it — the majority convention. The finding survives only because of the *within-resource* asymmetry against three sibling writes and the 404-description asymmetry between create and update. Had I stopped at the omission I would have filed a false positive, and the reason I nearly did is ordering: I built the claim first and went looking for the control afterward. The control has to run first.

`[PARKED]` `app.alfaview.com` as a second API surface — closed negative. `302 → /` → SPA shell.

`[PARKED]` Mass assignment — class closed; all 21 request-body schemas `additionalProperties: false`.

`[PARKED]` Carried forward unchanged: 403-declaration asymmetry generally, two-tier 401, the false base64 message, the query-parameter binder, `roomTypes` doc defect, SSRF as a class, the `Participant`/`userShow` third tier.

`[FINAL]` sso.alfaview.com `/oauth2/introspect` — **94**
`[FINAL]` apis.alfaview.com `GET /v2/guest-links` company-wide list — **80** (reframed this turn: this is the *gated* half)
