`[LEARN]` REJECTED (self) @ apis.alfaview.com: I read a `409` as proof that the server validates participant↔room binding and used it to park a hypothesis, then as a *second* incorrect claim attributed that 409 to a "PATCH sibling" that is not on the same path. The 409 is documented "The resource already exists" — a duplicate-create guard. Reading a status number's meaning instead of its description in the same file is now the third distinct instance of inferring semantics from a token I recognise.

`[LEARN]` REJECTED (self) @ apis.alfaview.com: I built the "missing 403" finding before measuring how many GETs omit 403. The answer, 14 of 27, invalidated the version I would otherwise have filed. I ran a control on an absence **after** asserting the absence was anomalous, which is backwards, and the only reason it was caught is that I checked.

`[LEARN]` ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (negative) @ app.alfaview.com: `/v2/docs/openapi.json` → `302 Found. Redirecting to /` → SPA shell `200`. The API contract is published only at `apis.alfaview.com` and `beta-apis.alfaview.com`; there is no second API deployment on the app host, and the 37-path baseline is not recoverable there.

`[LEARN]` ACCEPTED OTHER (scope) @ scope.yml: `account_creation: restricted — only on test/sandbox assets, never live customer data`. My previous `[NEXT]` assumed a live company signup was available to me. It is not, without program sign-off. Also confirmed in force: `no_data_modification: true`, descriptive-error findings out of scope, and authentication-data exposure excluded — which is why every probe above records status and content-length only, and why I do not read a body even from my own tenant's link list.

## STEP 7 — RISK

`[RISK]` alfaview: 74 — down 2 from 76, and the decline is the honest reading. This turn produced two genuine findings (the guest/group twin-pair asymmetry at 70, the permissions read+create pair at 66) and also **two claims I had to retract**, one of which reversed a parking decision made an hour earlier on the strength of a status number I never read the description of. Three retractions in a single turn is the worst rate in this program, and the mechanism is now consistent enough to name: I keep inferring behaviour from recognisable tokens — a status code, a path substring, an absent response — instead of reading the description and operationId that the contract supplies next to them. A missing `403` was going to be filed as a vulnerability; the 14/27 base rate killed it, and it was caught only because I went looking for the control *after* building the claim. That ordering is the defect, and the control has to run before the claim next time.

Also correcting a scope error of my own: scope.yml:44 sets `account_creation: restricted — only on test/sandbox assets`, and my previous `[NEXT] HUMAN` treated a live company signup as available to me. It is not. Any account I obtain has to be a sandbox or explicitly sanctioned, which makes the next step slower and slightly less certain, and I should not have proposed it as a routine step.

What is genuinely better: the standing guest-link finding is now *sharper* rather than merely larger, because the twin-pair comparison shows its 403 is real and locates the ungated half — group links — which the previous 500-record-per-page framing never separated out. The `participantId` primitive is confirmed intra-tenant, which removes the two-tenant blocker I had wrongly treated as disqualifying and makes it a one-account, one-room test.

What I will not claim: neither new finding has any wire evidence. Both are statements about what a contract *declares*, and in both cases the most likely real-world outcome is that the spec is imprecise rather than the code is unguarded — the 14/27 base rate is direct evidence that this spec omits 403 casually, and a 403 that the spec forgets to document is a 403 that still fires. A missing response in an OpenAPI document is a documentation defect on its own terms, and the program explicitly excludes descriptive-error findings, so the honest framing for both is: *the contract documents a control on one half of a pair and not the other, and a token is required to establish which half the enforcement layer actually gates.* The 409 and 403 asymmetries I reported last turn as findings were, on inspection, mostly artifacts of reading statuses instead of text.

The program's shape is unchanged: seven surviving findings, every one 401-gated, and not one authorization behaviour measured. Twenty-eight requests this turn across two hosts, all GET/HEAD, ≤1 rps, no credentials, no accounts created, no mutating verb, no body read. The bottleneck is one sandbox account, and it now requires a human with program access.
