locked out of the API, and the only remaining API access is a per-user API key — while an account that has NOT enabled
2FA still authenticates natively with no second factor and no IdP involvement. The realistic trigger is migration: a
company on native accounts later enables SSO with a 2FA-enforcing IdP and reasonably assumes 2FA now governs all access,
while the pre-existing native credentials keep working against the first-party API. This is the well-known
"SSO without disabling local auth" class.
