## STEP 6 — LEARNING

```
[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com GET /v2/audit-log (confidence 20, prior claim retracted): a live
  39th path absent from the 38-path contract and the entire 37-cycle recon snapshot, whose pre-auth binder is
  fully measured (from/to required date-time, limit 1..50, actionType enum read|write; pageToken, outcome,
  actorType, targetType, sort do not bind). It is NOT an orphan and NOT ungated: it mirrors GraphQL
  listAuditEvents on app.alfaview.com/graphql, and the audit log is gated by backend permission 21 (AUDIT_LOG),
  a child of ROOT 16 (MANAGE_COMPANY), plus a client flag limited to two alfaview-internal training companies.
  Both entry points return UNAUTHENTICATED with valid parameters. Auth gate and permission axis measured;
  company-scope enforcement unmeasured.
[LEARN] REJECTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com GET /v2/audit-log: "the contract contains no permission axis able
  to gate the audit log, therefore the audit log is ungated." Falsified by the client. ACCESS_ABILITIES.auditLog
  is bound to numeric backend permission 21 via v$(backendPermissions, [PERMISSION_TYPES.AUDIT_LOG]), and
  PARENT_TO_CHILDREN_MAP puts 21 under ROOT 16. Generalisable lesson, and the second time this program has
  drawn it: an absence in the published OpenAPI is evidence about the documentation, never about the control.
  Read the client, or read a token, before asserting a missing permission.
[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ app.alfaview.com: "an undocumented GraphQL surface with __typename is a
  weakness." Rejected. Apollo Server with INTROSPECTION_DISABLED, a CSRF guard requiring a non-form content-type
  plus apollo-require-preflight or x-apollo-operation-name, and UNAUTHENTICATED on the audit resolver. The
  correct framing is that this surface exists and is undocumented, not that it is exposed.
[LEARN] REJECTED SURFACE @ beta-app.alfaview.com, staging-app.alfaview.com: /graphql returns 401 on GET and on
  POST with correct headers. The business-logic GraphQL service is production-only. Recorded to stop the next
  cycle trying it.
[LEARN] REJECTED SURFACE @ apis.alfaview.com: /graphql 404/19B. The audit data is reachable on apis only via
  the REST path /v2/audit-log; the GraphQL path lives on app.alfaview.com.
[LEARN] REJECTED SURFACE @ apis.alfaview.com: /v2/audit-log/{uuid} and /v2/audit-log/{metadata,
  metadata-attributes, searchable-metadata, attributes} all 404/19B. No detail route, no metadata-attribute
  route under this prefix. The Event.metadata description's named "List Searchable metadata attributes"
  operation does not exist: 16 candidate GraphQL root-field names all returned Cannot query field, and no
  other root field on Query mentions metadata. Treat that description as a stale cross-reference.
[LEARN] METHOD: Apollo Server's per-field validation errors are a complete schema oracle with no auth and no
  data. `Field "X" is not defined by type "Y". Did you mean "Z"?` enumerates input fields, scalar types
  (int-literal probes reveal String), object subfields, and the nearest real neighbours of an absent name. Full
  AuditEvent/AuditActor/AuditTarget/filter/time-range type surface recovered in ~40 unauthenticated queries at
  ≤1.1 rps. This beats OpenAPI diffing for Go services that ship no introspection, and it costs no credentials.
  Introspection stays disabled, which is exactly what makes the validation-error channel worth knowing.
[LEARN] METHOD: the client bundle is a permission-model source, not just a route-string source. Numeric
  BACKEND_PERMISSIONS, ROOT_PERMISSION_ID, PARENT_TO_CHILDREN_MAP and the
  ACCESS_ABILITIES ← v$(backendPermissions,[PERMISSION_TYPES.X]) bindings reconstruct the server's
  authorization model offline, with no account and no customer data. Use it to predict a gate before spending
  an authenticated request, and to discard hypotheses whose permission axis turns out to exist.
```

