6557:[NEW] apis.alfaview.com TokenUserPermissions is a CLOSED vocabulary: additionalProperties:false, all 7 fields required = manageCompany, roomAdmin, roomCreate, roomList, userAdmin, userList, userShow. There is no guest-link or group-link axis. InviteUserPermissions is the identical 7-field set. The platform's own convention is that a company-wide list gets a named permission (roomList="list all rooms in their company", userList="list all users in their company") — the guest-link list is the one company-wide read whose sensitivity exceeds any published permission, and GET /v2/guest-links nevertheless declares 403 "The requesting user does not have the necessary permissions" while GET /v2/group-links does not.
6576:reasoning: GET /v2/guest-links declares only pageToken and limit — no roomId, no companyId, no filter — while the room-scoped sibling /v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links exists separately, so the unscoped list is a strict superset with no narrowing parameter. Every listed object is a GuestLink whose required fields include accessKey, joinUrl, dialInCode, emailAddress and roomId. The only documented mechanism for gating a company-wide read is the token's TokenUserPermissions, which is additionalProperties:false with all 7 fields required (manageCompany, roomAdmin, roomCreate, roomList, userAdmin, userList, userShow); no guest-link axis exists, and InviteUserPermissions is the identical 7-field set. The platform does gate its other company-wide lists by name in exactly this vocabulary (roomList = "list all rooms in their company", userList = "list all users in their company"), so the convention exists and is not applied here. Guest links are otherwise permission-bearing principals: RoomPermissions carries guestLinkPermissions[] and groupLinkPermissions[] of ParticipantPermissions, and PermissionsEdit.admin is "manage guests in the room" — so the read that discloses the credential behind those principals is gated by nothing expressible. The credential is directly redeemable: POST /v2/auth/guest-link takes {companyId, roomId, accessKey} (displayName not required, server-confirmed) and returns GuestLinkAuthenticationInfo, whose accessToken field is documented as empty only when the link is "not yet / no longer valid"; the handler demonstrably performs the lookup, returning the post-lookup business tier 422/88B ACTION_INVALID for well-formed synthetic values. Every probe in this cycle returned 401/107B pre-auth, so no authorization behavior is claimed. The spec's declaration of 403 on GET /v2/guest-links (absent on GET /v2/group-links) shows a check is contemplated, but a declared response code is not a named permission and I proved this cycle that this spec under-declares auth codes.
6616:[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: the company-wide guest-link list is un-narrowable and credential-bearing, and the published contract contains no permission able to express the check. Two independent halves, both now fully specified. (a) Scope: ListGuestLinks takes only pageToken and limit, the room-scoped sibling /v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links exists separately, so the unscoped list is a strict superset with no filter, and its 12 required fields include accessKey, joinUrl, dialInCode and guest emailAddress. (b) Authority: the only documented gate is TokenUserPermissions, additionalProperties:false with all 7 fields required (manageCompany, roomAdmin, roomCreate, roomList, userAdmin, userList, userShow) — no guest-link axis, and InviteUserPermissions is the identical 7-field set. The platform does name a permission for every other company-wide list it documents (roomList = "list all rooms in their company", userList = "list all users in their company"), so the convention exists and is not applied to the one read that returns live join credentials. Guest links are otherwise first-class permission-bearing principals: RoomPermissions carries guestLinkPermissions[] and groupLinkPermissions[] of ParticipantPermissions, and PermissionsEdit.admin is "edit or delete rooms, change room settings or features and manage guests in the room". The credential is directly redeemable — POST /v2/auth/guest-link takes {companyId, roomId, accessKey}, displayName not required (server-confirmed 422/171B differential), and GuestLinkAuthenticationInfo.accessToken is documented as empty only when the link is "not yet / no longer valid"; the handler demonstrably performs its lookup, returning the post-lookup business tier 422/88B ACTION_INVALID for well-formed synthetic values. Both halves are provable from the published contract with zero credentials. Neither is proven on the wire: this cycle established that the edge proxy makes the question unreachable without a token, so authorization behaviour is entirely unmeasured, and the server may enforce an undeclared check or the live token-info response may carry fields the spec omits.
6629:reasoning: Two independently sufficient halves, both now exact. Scope: ListGuestLinks declares parameters Authorization, pageToken, limit(int32, default 100, max 500) and nothing else — no roomId, no companyId, no filter, no sort — while the room-scoped sibling /v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links exists separately, so the unscoped list is a strict superset that cannot be narrowed through the documented contract. Its envelope PaginatedBodyListGuestLink is additionalProperties:false, required:[data], {data: GuestLink[]|null, nextPageToken}, with no totalCount, so the company cannot even be sized from outside. Payload: GuestLink is additionalProperties:false with 12 of 15 fields required — id, roomId, createdBy, createdAt, permissionGroupId, accessKey, emailAddress, locale, origin, dialInAllowed, dialInCode, joinUrl — so a 200 is contractually guaranteed to carry the join credential and the guest's email address for every object returned, and cannot omit them as a naive implementation would. GroupLink is the same shape at 12 of 14 with joinLink added and emailAddress absent, confirming shared non-per-guest links. dialInCode is required unconditionally beside the required boolean dialInAllowed, so the telephony PIN is returned even for links where dialing is switched off. Authority: the only documented gate is TokenUserPermissions, additionalProperties:false with all 7 fields required — manageCompany, roomAdmin, roomCreate, roomList, userAdmin, userList, userShow — containing no guest-link or group-link axis, and InviteUserPermissions is the identical 7-field set. The convention is not absent: roomList is documented as "list all rooms in their company" and userList as "list all users in their company", so every other company-wide read in this API is name-gated and this one is not. Guest links are nonetheless first-class permission-bearing principals — RoomPermissions carries guestLinkPermissions[] and groupLinkPermissions[] of ParticipantPermissions{participantId, permissions}, and PermissionsEdit.admin covers "manage guests in the room" — so the read that discloses the credential behind those principals is gated by nothing expressible. Redemption completes the chain: POST /v2/auth/guest-link takes {companyId, roomId, accessKey} and, unlike its group-link sibling, does not require displayName (server-confirmed via 422/171B vs 422/88B differential), and GuestLinkAuthenticationInfo.accessToken is documented as empty only when the link is "not yet / no longer valid"; the handler demonstrably performs the lookup, returning the post-lookup business tier 422/88B ACTION_INVALID for well-formed synthetic triples. This cycle established the boundary of what can be known: the edge proxy rejects before query and path validation, proven by roomId=abc and /v2/rooms/abc/guest-links both returning the identical 401/107B as the well-formed control, with ?bogusParam=abc confirming no parameter discrimination. Therefore a hidden-but-accepted filter is unknowable without a token, and authorization behaviour is entirely unmeasured. A 403 declared on ListGuestLinks and absent on ListGroupLinks shows a check is contemplated, but declarations here are demonstrably not behaviourally load-bearing.
6688:reasoning: The contract half is unchanged and exact: `ListGuestLinks` declares parameters `Authorization` (header), `pageToken` (string) and `limit` (int32, default 100, max 500) and nothing else, no filter, no sort, no include; the room-scoped sibling `/v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links` exists separately, so the unscoped list is a strict superset; the envelope `PaginatedBodyListGuestLink` is `additionalProperties:false`, `required:[data]`, shaped `{data, nextPageToken}` with no `totalCount`, so the company cannot even be sized from outside. The payload half: `GuestLink` is `additionalProperties:false` with 12 of 15 fields required including `accessKey`, `joinUrl`, `dialInCode` and `emailAddress`; `dialInCode` is required unconditionally beside the required boolean `dialInAllowed`, so a dialing-disabled link still returns its telephony PIN. **New this cycle, the behavioural half.** `/v2/guest-links?roomId=abc&limit=abc` → 422/141B and `?companyId=abc&limit=abc` → 422/141B, both md5 `551221c3`, each returning an `errors` array with exactly one entry, `query.limit`. Because that array is a complete per-parameter enumerator — proven by `?limit=abc` alone producing the same single entry, and by `/v2/rooms?roomTypes=abc` producing a correctly indexed `query.roomTypes[0]` — the absence of a `roomId` and a `companyId` entry is positive evidence that the server does not bind them, not merely evidence that the docs omit them. The authority half is unchanged: `TokenUserPermissions` is `additionalProperties:false` with all seven fields required — `manageCompany`, `roomAdmin`, `roomCreate`, `roomList`, `userAdmin`, `userList`, `userShow` — and has no guest-link or group-link axis, while `roomList` and `userList` are each documented as "list all rooms/users in their company", so the naming convention exists and is applied to every other company-wide read but not to the one that returns live join credentials. `ListGuestLinks` declares 403, `ListGroupLinks` does not. Redemption is the documented unauthenticated `POST /v2/auth/guest-link` taking `{companyId, roomId, accessKey}` with `displayName` not required, and the handler demonstrably performs its lookup, returning the post-lookup 422/88B `ACTION_INVALID` tier for well-formed synthetic values.
6859:asset: apis.alfaview.com — `InviteUserPermissions` (body of `POST /v2/users/invitation`) vs `TokenUserPermissions` (200 of `GET /v2/auth/token-info`)
6861:reasoning: Verified programmatically. Both schemas have **exactly identical field sets** — 7 booleans, all required, `additionalProperties:false` — {manageCompany, roomAdmin, roomCreate, roomList, userAdmin, userList, userShow}. Descriptions diverge: `InviteUserPermissions` documents three implications; `TokenUserPermissions` documents **none** (zero "Implies" occurrences). `InviteUserPermissions` is referenced exactly once in the whole contract, as `UserInvitation.permissions`; `TokenUserPermissions` exactly once, as `TokenInfo.permissions` — and is **never** accepted as a request body, so no API exposes writing a token's permission set. The platform can therefore *express* `{manageCompany: true, userShow: false}` at invitation time (the implication is prose, not a schema constraint) and *observe* it via token-info, with the enforcement layer's interpretation undocumented. This also sharpens the standing `userShow` hypothesis: `userShow` is the only field whose documented meaning is participant-identity disclosure, and it is one of the three `manageCompany` is documented to imply — so the two documented resolution rules disagree about whether it is independently controllable. That disagreement is exactly what a per-field gate on `GET /v2/rooms/{id}/participants` must adjudicate.
