6864:impact: If enforcement resolves implications from `TokenUserPermissions` (which documents none) rather than `InviteUserPermissions` (which documents three), a user the invitation layer treats as a full company manager may be denied participant-identity reads — or, damagingly, a narrowly-granted user may inherit the participant list through an implication applied opposite to the one the invitation schema documents. The asset at risk is `GET /v2/rooms/{id}/participants`, whose payload is the internal `userId` UUID that `DELETE /v2/users/{id}` and `PATCH /v2/rooms/{roomId}/permissions/{userId}` consume as their sole input. MEDIUM and honestly speculative: this is a documentation-semantics divergence, not a demonstrated defect, and it is entirely plausible the server treats the 7 booleans as independent, in which case nothing is wrong.
6940:verify_steps: Passive portion complete. Decisive, for a human with a test tenant: (1) invite via `POST /v2/users/invitation` with all 7 booleans and `{"manageCompany": true, "userShow": false}` — a write, needs sign-off. (2) as that user `GET /v2/auth/token-info`, record `permissions` verbatim. (3) `GET /v2/rooms/<own-room>/participants` — record status only, count entries, never read identity values. A 200 under `userShow:false` confirms the gate is not enforced.
