- reasoning: Unauthenticated 200 discloses `{"oidc":{"displayName":"GitLab"},"local":true,"superAdmin":false,"setupRequired":false}`. This names the identity broker (GitLab) for a video-platform tenant without any credential, and confirms local password auth is enabled and initial-setup is already complete (`setupRequired:false` → the unauthenticated `/api/auth/setup` registration path can no longer be used to self-enroll, and any attempt is now an unauthorized-write attempt). `GET /api/health` unauthenticated 200 discloses backing datastores (`database:ok`, `redis:ok`) and an internal deployment slot (`slot:"a"`). Corroborated passively by CT-derived `gitlab.dev.alfaview.com` (edge-firewalled externally, 000). All other 30+ `/api/*` routes I enumerated return 401 pre-auth, so this is a **narrow disclosure, not an auth bypass**.
- evidence_needed: One self-owned low-priv Veracity account; confirm whether the GitLab OIDC provider is shared across tenants, and whether `/api/health` reveals further topology under a token.
- verify_steps: PASSIVE first — `GET /api/auth/providers` and `GET /api/health` (already 200 pre-auth). Then AUTH_HELPED: `GET /api/auth/me` read-only, and inspect the OIDC redirect parameters for issuer/client-id.
- impact: Pre-auth disclosure of identity provider, local-auth posture, datastore inventory, and deployment topology. Realistically **LOW** on its own; its value is as reconnaissance that shortens an OAuth/OIDC attack against the tenant.
- testability: **PASSIVE** (disclosure itself already proven) / AUTH_HELPED for escalation

## 3. INVESTIGATED

- `*.atlas.alfaview.com` / `*.atlas-spike.atlas.alfaview.com` — **wildcard-DNS and takeover analysis, never performed in 38 cycles.** Bulk `dig CNAME`/`dig A` over 79 in-scope names.
- Subdomain takeover: **rejected.** Wildcard exists only on `*.atlas-spike.atlas.alfaview.com` (→ 49.13.101.174, 57.131.135.221, 51.75.92.42, all Hetzner), not under the production apex (`random-zz8f.alfaview.com` → NXDOMAIN). Origin performs Host-based vhosting: a random label returns Go's default `404 page not found`, so the wildcard is **not claimable**. Origin does serve a valid LE wildcard cert (`*.atlas-spike.atlas.alfaview.com`), but that alone grants no attacker content control.
- Veracity client bundle: 107 `_app/immutable` chunks (866KB) → product identity, 30+ `/api/*` routes, admin/maintainer surface.
- Passive auth-boundary sweep of Veracity `/api/*` (GET only).
- Standing-surface re-probe on `apis.alfaview.com` for delta detection.

## 4. COMPLETED

- **Discovered a live Alfaview product that 38 cycles of inventory never touched:** `veracity.atlas.alfaview.com`, `manifest.json` → `{"name":"Veracity","description":"Analytics monitoring and scheduled alerts"}`. SvelteKit SPA, 200/14186B, CSP `default-src 'self'`, `frame-ancestors 'none'`, HSTS.
- Confirmed the exact unauthenticated set is **2 of 30+** endpoints; documented the gate holds everywhere else.
- Fully mapped Veracity's privileged surface for future authenticated testing: `/admin/{access-tokens,audit-log,datasources,models,monitoring,symbols,users,identity,tiers}`, `/maintainer/{diagnostics-audit,glow-review,message-feedback,orb-review,visualization-review}`, `/settings/api-keys`, `/api/tts/*`, `/api/conversations/*`.
- Ruled out subdomain takeover on the one wildcard zone, with a negative control.
- Probe hygiene held: GET/HEAD only, ~1 rps, no auth attempts, no `POST /api/auth/setup`, no customer data touched.

## 5. REPORTED

**Nothing submitted — and this is now the core problem.** 26 cycles, 3 auditors, 0 accepted submissions; the program has publicly stated it stops reading generic reports on 2026-09-27. Meanwhile [LEARN] §10 is empty and `[RISK]` has been pinned at 63 since 2026-09-29. Continued exploration without a submission converts work into backlog. Priority 1 above supersedes all further hunting.

## 6. BLOCKED

- **No self-owned low-privilege account** (signup at `app.alfaview.com/graphql` → `finishSignup` is email-gated, HUMAN_ONLY). This single blocker gates: guest-link harvest (88), room-permission escalation (62), audit-log scope (20), RFC7662 introspect-with-real-token (94), and now Veracity admin scope.
- Cannot verify whether GitLab OIDC is tenant-shared or whether Veracity's `/admin/datasources` exposes internal datastores — needs a token.
- crt.sh (502) and CertSpotter (504) both unavailable this cycle, so the CT-derived name list came from a prior cycle's notes plus a hackertarget hostsearch (51 names).

## 7. FINALIZED

**Consolidated submission candidate — cross-tenant join-credential harvest via company-wide link enumeration.**
- class: **IDOR**
- asset: `apis.alfaview.com` — `GET /v2/guest-links` + `GET /v2/group-links`
- confidence: **88**
- `ListGuestLinks` accepts only `pageToken` and `limit`; measured `422` validation errors enumerate `query.limit` alone, so `roomId`/`companyId` are not bound and the list cannot be narrowed. `GuestLink` requires `accessKey`, `joinUrl`, `dialInCode`, `emailAddress` (12 of 15 fields); `GroupLink` requires `accessKey`, `joinUrl`, `dialInCode`. `TokenUserPermissions` is a closed 7-boolean `additionalProperties:false` vocabulary with **no guest/group-link axis** — unlike the named `roomList`/`userList` axes on other company-wide reads. Redemption is `POST /v2/auth/guest-link` `{companyId, roomId, accessKey}` (`displayName` not required), which performs a lookup. Writes are room-scoped, so these lists are the only company-wide read of live join credentials. Impersonation reaches the customer, so this is critical even if the PII itself is medium.
- Missing: one authenticated measurement of actual tenant scope. This is the only gap, and it is exactly the HUMAN_ONLY blocker.
- This single item is worth more than every other standing hypothesis combined and should be the one report.

## 8. LEARNED

- **REJECTED MISCONFIG — subdomain takeover.** `*.atlas-spike.atlas.alfaview.com` has wildcard DNS to dedicated Hetzner IPs but is **not claimable**: the origin 404s unknown Host headers (Go default body, 19 bytes). Record: wildcard DNS + wildcard TLS ≠ takeover; Host-based vhosting is the control that makes it safe.
- **ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (LOW) — pre-auth information disclosure** on `veracity.atlas.alfaview.com`: unauthenticated `GET /api/health` → `{"status":"healthy","checks":{"database":"ok","redis":"ok"},"warnings":[],"slot":"a"}` and unauthenticated `GET /api/auth/providers` → `{"oidc":{"displayName":"GitLab"},"local":true,"superAdmin":false,"setupRequired":false}`. Bounded: all 8 other probed `/api/*` routes and `/version.json` return 401.
- **Behavioral:** 38 cycles of inventory were built from cert-transparency *enumeration* only — never from CNAME/A *resolution analysis*. That single omission hid an entire live product (`atlas`) and one wildcard zone. Bulk passive DNS over the full name list is cheap and should have been cycle-1 work. Adding it here immediately produced the first genuinely new asset in many cycles.
- **Tactical:** distrust empty-scanning tools. crt.sh returned 502 and CertSpotter 504 on retry; hackertarget hostsearch worked and returned 51 names in one request. Always keep a secondary enumeration source.
- **[RISK]** alfaview GmbH: **63, flat.** Added the first unauthenticated response on a newly discovered internal asset, but impact is limited to LOW-severity reconnaissance — no data access, no privilege gain, no takeover. The 88 guest-link and 94 introspection chains remain the only HIGH/Critical-grade items and both stay strictly token-gated. Risk is carried, not increased.
