- 2026-09-28 ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: the participant-identity read is the program's first *self-serving* IDOR oracle. Every prior chain in this program has been blocked on a missing input — a token, an account, a user ID. This endpoint hands out the user ID. `GET /v2/rooms/{id}/participants` returns, for every principal currently connected to a room and bucketed by `subRoomId`, the internal `userId` UUID plus `displayName`, `isGuest`, `isDialIn`, `onBreak`, `inWaitingRoom` and `isSpectator` — and those UUIDs are the sole input to `DELETE /v2/users/{id}` and `PATCH /v2/rooms/{roomId}/permissions/{userId}`, the two unscoped destructive operations this program has carried as its standing IDOR target with no way to supply an identifier. The vendor documents the gate for exactly this data and nowhere else: `TokenUserPermissions.userShow` is "If true, the user can see the user names of all users in rooms they can access", in a closed 7-boolean all-required vocabulary whose other six fields are about company/room/user administration and bear no relation to participant identity. Exhaustive reference search confirms **no operation in the 57-operation contract references `TokenUserPermissions` except `GetTokenInfo` returning it** — so the enforcement point is undocumented and this operation is the only plausible consumer. Three independent corroborations that alfaview considers participant identity sensitive: `RoomFeatures` ships `allowCopyRoomInfo` ("Allows moderators to copy the contents of the participant list to the clipboard"), `showRaisedHandsOnlyToModerators` and `shareContentOnlyToModerators`, and `examScreenshare` shares every participant's monitors with moderators. The room-level `Permissions` vocabulary is a closed 9-boolean set with **no** participant-visibility axis, so there is no in-room way to withhold list access; `userShow` is the only expressible gate. The severity hinge is token class: `POST /v2/auth/guest-link` is unauthenticated, takes only `{companyId, roomId, accessKey}`, and returns a real `accessToken` whenever the link is valid (the spec marks it "Can be empty if the guest link is not yet / no longer valid" — empty only when invalid), so an invitation URL handed to an external third party is a plausible route to employees' and moderators' internal UUIDs with no company account involved. Confidence 78, `AUTH_HELPED`, **zero wire evidence of a defect claimed** — the contract shape and the single documented gate are measured; the authorization behaviour is unmeasured.
- 2026-09-28 ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com — methodological, and it reverses a conclusion I reached mid-cycle: I first read the missing-403 audit as evidence that the participant read was *ungated*, then inverted it. The 403 split across 9 room-scoped GETs is 4 with (participants, attendances, group-links, guest-links) and 5 without (room, features, language-settings, permissions, subrooms) — so 403-declaration draws a line around credential, attendance and identity reads and deliberately not around room configuration. The defensible claim is therefore **fit, not uniqueness**: `userShow` is the only permission in the contract whose documented meaning is this endpoint's payload, and `token-info` makes the test a binary read of the caller's own bit rather than an inference. I am recording the reversal because the specific error — generalising a control from an absence in a declaration table rather than from a behavioural measurement — is the same class of error I retracted in each of the last two cycles, and it would have produced an overstated finding in the same direction both times.
- 2026-09-28 ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (negative result) @ apis.alfaview.com: the contract's structural change produced **zero** new pre-authentication exposure. All 11 newly-surfaced paths return 401/107B md5 `60ed2f29` — `/v2/users/me/company` and the 10 room-scoped reads including the never-before-probed `participants`, `features`, `file-share-limits`, `file-share-settings`, `language-settings`, `passcode` and `subrooms` — and malformed identifiers (`not-a-uuid`) still return 401 on both a declared GET and a declared 401-only route, so the UUID path validator still runs behind authentication. The 27-GET sweep left the pre-auth validator map unchanged at 9 operations, of which only `/v2/stats` (422/319B md5 6d812e83) and `/v2/rooms/{roomId}/attendances` (422/232B md5 b4adcd15) disclose per-field names. The new schemas are individually unremarkable for attack surface: `RoomSettingsUpdate` is `additionalProperties:false` with only `defaultAccessLevel`, `enabled` and `participantsCanUpload`, closing mass assignment on that route; `RoomLimits` is read-only quota disclosure; `Passcode` returns `passcode` plus `required` and is 401-gated; `RoomFeaturesEdit` is `additionalProperties:false` with 14 nullable tri-state fields and no non-boolean injection surface; the subroom family is correctly room-scoped on both PATCH and DELETE, and `subrooms/{subroomId}` confirms `allow: DELETE, PATCH` exactly as declared.
- 2026-09-28 ACCEPTED MISCONFIG (negative result, and it downgrades a standing hypothesis) @ apis.alfaview.com: the unscoped-resource-update defect is **not** a platform pattern. I tested the six newly-surfaced schema families for unscoped siblings — the exact shape already confirmed on `/v2/guest-links/{id}` and `/v2/group-links/{id}` — with 9 HEAD probes: `/v2/subrooms`, `/v2/subrooms/{id}`, `/v2/permissions/{id}`, `/v2/room-features`, `/v2/passcode`, `/v2/attendances`, `/v2/participants`, `/v2/file-share-settings`, `/v2/room-permissions`. All nine returned 404/19B. Combined with the `Allow:`-versus-spec check matching the contract exactly on all 11 new paths, the undocumented `DELETE` on `/v2/users/invitations` now looks like a single instance rather than the first of many, which is the pre-registered upgrade condition for the 63 route-enumeration finding and was not met. The 405 branch is therefore parked, and I am explicitly declining to spend further cycles extending a reconnaissance primitive when three HIGH chains are blocked on one absent account.
