Cycle complete. Key result: the introspection bypass is not universal — it's gated on token shape.

**The finding.** `sso.alfaview.com/oauth2/introspect` validates `client_id` only when the token is a 3-segment JWT-shaped string whose second segment base64-decodes to a JSON object. Every other shape returns `200 {"active":false}` with an arbitrary `client_id` and no `client_secret`, on both POST-body and HTTP Basic channels. Pinned with3-4 repeats per boundary case after a single trial contradicted an earlier reading.

```
token=zzz.<b64 {"sub":"1"}>.sig + client_id=NOPE -> 401 invalid_client_id
token=<opaque 24/32/48B b64>    + client_id=NOPE  -> 200 {"active":false}
token=zzz..sig / a.<obj>.c.d / seg2=[1]/123/null -> 200 (unchecked)
seg2={} / {"a":1} / {"a":{}}   / seg1="" -> 401 (checked)
```

**Why it matters more than the prior 94.** alfaview's own API tokens are opaque base64, not JWTs (`AccessToken` schema says "base64-encoded string"; `/v2/auth/token-info` rejects every JWT variant with 422). Opaque tokens fall on the unchecked path. So a leaked alfaview token yields full token-state disclosure to someone holding zero client credentials. The token endpoint validates `client_id` for every grant, which makes this an anomaly rather than a global misconfig.

**Other deltas.** `/v2/audit-log` is a live 39th path absent from the spec (422 → 401, six sibling guesses404). 20 of 57 operations document no 403, including `GET /v2/guest-links` documenting 403 while the isomorphic `GET /v2/group-links` does not — new hypothesis at 58. `/v2/docs/openapi.yaml` is a byte-equivalent second publication.

**Retraction.** The parked `device_authorize` claim is false — re-probed, returns `401 invalid_client_id`. Prior reasoning was also wrong about the introspect *mechanism*, though not the bypass.

**Blocker unchanged.** All three top chains need an authorized account plus an opaque token. Risk 88 (up 1). Details in `leads/lead-bigpickle.md`.
