6585:reasoning: The live asset app.min.67e8a68d4318b34ca241.js (1092529B, md5 2cb9128353b1f7444e222b4b61e4ffa5) contains `mutation AdminSwitchCompany($nextCompanyId: String!) { adminSwitchCompany(nextCompanyId: $nextCompanyId) { companyId accessToken permissions } }` and its call site `{query:AdminSwitchCompany,headers:{token:d},variables:{nextCompanyId:n}}`. The only credential transmitted is the caller's existing token; the target company is a bare String argument, so the server must decide administration of nextCompanyId itself and the client offers it no evidence either way. The reply is a fresh accessToken plus that company's permissions, i.e. the operation is a token-minting cross-tenant boundary crossing. The same bundle hardcodes AV_COMPANY_ID="alfatraining-internal" and the 26-character company identifier 01FDY0986YK1BJF2K0F9DXR8EB, which matches the companyId format the REST API documents (APICredentials.companyId example 0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOP), so the reconnaissance an attacker needs is already public. The admin surface is small and now fully mapped: adminTokenAuthenticate, adminSwitchCompany, adminReinvitePendingUser, getFinishSignupLinkAsAdmin(companyId, username) — two of the four take a caller-supplied companyId. adminAccessToken is persisted to localStorage at two write sites, so any XSS anywhere on the admin origin yields the highest-value token in the program; I found no XSS and claim none. No admin credential literal is baked into the asset. Anonymous reachability is untested and is the entire open question.
