849:- 2026-09-27 ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: the guest-link surface has no expressible authorization primitive. Guest links are first-class permission-bearing principals (RoomPermissions.guestLinkPermissions, PermissionsEdit.admin="manage guests in the room"), yet the closed 7-field company-scope token vocabulary (TokenUserPermissions) has no guest/group-link axis, and the one company-wide read that returns accessKey + joinUrl + dialInCode + guest emailAddress for every guest in the company has no roomId or companyId parameter to narrow it. Provable from the published contract with no credential. Not proven on the wire: the server may enforce an undeclared check, and the live token-info response may carry fields the spec omits — that is the single decisive read.
852:- 2026-09-27 ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com: the company-wide guest-link list is un-narrowable and credential-bearing, and the published contract contains no permission able to express the check. Two independent halves, both now fully specified. (a) Scope: ListGuestLinks takes only pageToken and limit, the room-scoped sibling /v2/rooms/{roomId}/guest-links exists separately, so the unscoped list is a strict superset with no filter, and its 12 required fields include accessKey, joinUrl, dialInCode and guest emailAddress. (b) Authority: the only documented gate is TokenUserPermissions, additionalProperties:false with all 7 fields required (manageCompany, roomAdmin, roomCreate, roomList, userAdmin, userList, userShow) — no guest-link axis, and InviteUserPermissions is the identical 7-field set. The platform does name a permission for every other company-wide list it documents (roomList = "list all rooms in their company", userList = "list all users in their company"), so the convention exists and is not applied to the one read that returns live join credentials. Guest links are otherwise first-class permission-bearing principals: RoomPermissions carries guestLinkPermissions[] and groupLinkPermissions[] of ParticipantPermissions, and PermissionsEdit.admin is "edit or delete rooms, change room settings or features and manage guests in the room". The credential is directly redeemable — POST /v2/auth/guest-link takes {companyId, roomId, accessKey}, displayName not required (server-confirmed 422/171B differential), and GuestLinkAuthenticationInfo.accessToken is documented as empty only when the link is "not yet / no longer valid"; the handler demonstrably performs its lookup, returning the post-lookup business tier 422/88B ACTION_INVALID for well-formed synthetic values. Both halves are provable from the published contract with zero credentials. Neither is proven on the wire: this cycle established that the edge proxy makes the question unreachable without a token, so authorization behaviour is entirely unmeasured, and the server may enforce an undeclared check or the live token-info response may carry fields the spec omits.
890:- 2026-09-28 ACCEPTED IDOR @ apis.alfaview.com — methodological, and it reverses a conclusion I reached mid-cycle: I first read the missing-403 audit as evidence that the participant read was *ungated*, then inverted it. The 403 split across 9 room-scoped GETs is 4 with (participants, attendances, group-links, guest-links) and 5 without (room, features, language-settings, permissions, subrooms) — so 403-declaration draws a line around credential, attendance and identity reads and deliberately not around room configuration. The defensible claim is therefore **fit, not uniqueness**: `userShow` is the only permission in the contract whose documented meaning is this endpoint's payload, and `token-info` makes the test a binary read of the caller's own bit rather than an inference. I am recording the reversal because the specific error — generalising a control from an absence in a declaration table rather than from a behavioural measurement — is the same class of error I retracted in each of the last two cycles, and it would have produced an overstated finding in the same direction both times.
1146:- 2026-10-05 ACCEPTED AUTH: alfaview API access tokens are opaque base64, not JWTs (AccessToken schema states "base64-encoded string"; /v2/auth/token-info rejects every JWT variant with 422). The introspect bypass therefore applies to exactly the token type the product issues, which raises its real-world exploitability from "token-gated" to "any leaked token".
